---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioJoiner from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioJoiner.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="audio-joiner">
  <GifAudioJoiner client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to join audio files</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add all files at once. Each one gets a waveform, drawn by the audio decoder of your browser.</li>
      <li>Reorder with the arrow buttons. The list plays from top to bottom.</li>
      <li>Drag the handles under a waveform to trim that track. The tool uses the highlighted section.</li>
      <li>Choose a crossfade if you want the tracks to blend.</li>
      <li>Join, listen to the result in the player, and download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Two paths, and why it matters which one runs</h2>
    <p>
      Joining audio can be lossless or lossy. This audio joiner picks the path from your settings and
      tells you which one it used.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Lossless copy</th><th>Re-encode</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Runs when</td><td>All files share one format, no trims, no crossfade</td><td>Mixed formats, any trim, or any crossfade</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happens</td><td>Audio packets are copied straight into the output container</td><td>Everything is decoded, normalised to 44.1 kHz stereo and encoded once</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quality</td><td>Bit-identical to the sources</td><td>One more generation of lossy encoding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Near-instant, even for hours of audio</td><td>About ten times faster than real time on a modern laptop</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If quality matters more than a smooth transition (audiobook chapters, or a recording split across
      files), first convert all files to the same format with the
      <a href="/audio-converter">audio converter</a>. Then join without trims to keep the lossless path.
    </p>

    <h2>How crossfades change the length</h2>
    <p>
      A crossfade overlaps two tracks, so it makes the result shorter. With <var>n</var> tracks and a
      crossfade of <var>d</var> seconds, the total is:
    </p>
    <p><code>total = sum of trimmed lengths − (n − 1) × d</code></p>
    <p>
      Four tracks of three minutes each with a two-second crossfade give 12 minutes minus 6 seconds.
      The running total above the track list includes this. A track shorter than the crossfade is
      rejected with an explanation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fade shape is <code>tri</code>, a linear triangular curve on both sides. It is the neutral
      default and suits speech, ambience and most music. Only sustained tonal material in matched keys
      gains from other shapes.
    </p>

    <h2>What the waveforms are for</h2>
    <p>
      The Web Audio API of the browser decodes each track, and the tool reduces it to 480 peak values,
      drawn as a bar chart. The highlighted part is what you keep; the dimmed parts are trimmed. You
      can see where the speech starts, cut the count-in at the start of a take, or trim the noise at
      the end of a voice note.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some browsers cannot decode all formats (Opus in Safari is the usual case). Those tracks show a
      note in place of a waveform. Trimming and joining still work without the picture.
    </p>

    <h2>Common jobs this handles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Stitching a split recording: phone recorders often split long sessions into 30-minute files. Join with no trims and no crossfade for one lossless file.</li>
      <li>Building a continuous mix: reorder, trim the silence off each end, add a two-second crossfade.</li>
      <li>Combining voice notes: turn a series of WhatsApp messages into one recording. To convert them first, see <a href="/opus-to-mp3">Opus to MP3</a>.</li>
      <li>Assembling an audiobook chapter: join the takes and trim the retakes at the boundaries.</li>
      <li>Adding an intro and outro to a podcast episode, with crossfades at both joins.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why 44.1 kHz stereo on the re-encode path</h2>
    <p>
      To concatenate and crossfade, all branches of the filter graph must have the same sample rate,
      sample format and channel layout, or FFmpeg cannot link them. This tool converts all branches to
      44.1 kHz stereo, the safest common format for playback. It does not copy the properties of the
      first file. A mono source is copied to both channels; a 48 kHz source is resampled once, with a
      high-quality filter.
    </p>

    <h2>No upload and no file cap</h2>
    <p>
      You can join thirty tracks per session. The only limit on their length or total size is the
      memory of your device, and there is no watermark or signup. Your processor does the work, so
      server limits do not apply. To confirm that nothing is sent, open the Network tab or turn off
      Wi-Fi first.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
